.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="H2PlusVariational builds the 2x2 Hamiltonian and overlap matrices for a minimal one-Gaussian- per-proton basis, solves the secular equation HC=SCE, and then variationally optimizes the shared Gaussian exponent -- a genuine application of the Rayleigh-Ritz variational theorem: minimizing over a real parameter can only lower (never raise) the computed energy toward the true ground state.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_h2_plus_variational_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_01_h2_plus_variational`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">H2+ in a minimal 2-Gaussian basis: the variational principle at work</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_01_h2_plus_variational`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Walter Heitler and Fritz London&#x27;s 1927 quantum-mechanical treatment of H2 -- the paper usually credited with founding quantum chemistry as a field -- showed for the first time why two hydrogen atoms bind: the symmetric (in-phase) combination of their two atomic orbitals builds up electron density in the region between the nuclei, lowering the energy below that of the separated atoms, while the antisymmetric (out-of-phase) combination depletes density there and raises the energy instead. Their original calculation used two-electron integrals this package does not implement (chemistrykit.quantum.utils.basis_sets stops at the one-electron integrals H2PlusVariational needs for H2+), but the same qualitative physics -- constructive vs. destructive interference of atomic orbitals along the bond axis -- is exactly what H2+&#x27;s own bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals show, using the genuine LCAO coefficients solve computes, not an assumed equal-weight combination.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_bonding_antibonding_density_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_02_bonding_antibonding_density`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Heitler-London's insight: bonding vs. antibonding electron density</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_02_bonding_antibonding_density`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Builds the Huckel secular matrix for butadiene and benzene (~chemistrykit.quantum.systems.huckel.HuckelSystem), diagonalizes it via numpy.linalg.eigh (a genuine eigenvalue problem, not a lookup table), cross-checks the result against the closed-form Coulson/Frost eigenvalue formulas, and checks Huckel&#x27;s 4n+2 rule (~chemistrykit.quantum.systems.huckel.is_aromatic_by_huckel_rule) against the computed spectrum&#x27;s degeneracies.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/huckel/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_huckel_theory_thumb.png
